# M6 — Frontend, Explainable Panel, Caching & Deployment

## Background

This is the final milestone for ShopRank (P1). The retrieval pipeline is fully operational with 5-row ablation, 3-locale cross-tab, and RRF diagnostics all complete. What remains: dimension ablation table, Angular UI with explainable panel, API surface hardening, demo cache precomputation, and deployment to production.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Execution order is fixed**: Part 1 (dimension ablation) kicks off first because it is pure machine time (~1–2 hours). Parts 2–4 proceed in parallel / sequentially while it runs.

## Open Questions

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Embedding cache issue**: The current disk cache in `.cache/embeddings/` stores **already-truncated 768-dim vectors** (filenames are `bge-m3_768_<hash>.npy`, shape `(768,)`). The `embed_documents()` function in [core/embeddings.py](file:///d:/Github_Clones/ShopRank/core/embeddings.py) calls the model, gets the raw (1024-dim) output, truncates to the requested `dim`, then caches the truncated result. **We cannot derive 1024-dim vectors from the cached 768-dim ones** — Matryoshka truncation is one-way.
>
> **Two options:**
> 1. **Re-run the model once at full 1024-dim** for all ~26k products, cache those, then derive 768 and 512 by pure truncation + L2 renormalization. Takes ~46 min on GPU. This is the correct Matryoshka approach.
> 2. **Re-run the model at each dim separately** (wasteful, ~2.3 hours total). This is what the current code would do if called naively.
>
> **My recommendation**: Option 1. We re-embed once at 1024-dim, cache those full vectors, then truncate in-memory for 768 and 512. The 768-dim results should be byte-identical to re-truncating from 1024 (Matryoshka guarantee). This also future-proofs the cache.

> [!NOTE]
> **SPEC discrepancy**: SPEC §22 mentions 1536-dim in one place. BGE-M3's native output is **1024-dim**. I will treat 1024 as correct and note the discrepancy in `docs/DECISIONS.md`.

